{"id":"h052","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Assume all gases are perfect unless stated otherwise. Note that 1 atm = 1.013 25 bar. Unless otherwise stated, thermochemical data are for 298.15 K. An average human produces about $10 \\mathrm{MJ}$ of heat each day through metabolic activity. If a human body were an isolated system of mass $65 \\mathrm{~kg}$ with the heat capacity of water, what temperature rise would the body experience?\nIs the correct answer \"+45$\\text{K}$\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct temperature rise is about +37 K, not +45 K.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"For an isolated body absorbing all the heat, ΔT = q/(mc).","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The daily heat produced is 10 MJ, or 1.0×10^7 J.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The body mass of 65 kg equals 6.5×10^4 g.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The specific heat of water is about 4.184 J/(g K).","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Substituting the values gives ΔT of about 37 K.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Treating the body as isolated and using water's heat capacity are reasonable assumptions.","confidence":0.7,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.0996,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"A real body loses heat by radiation, convection, evaporation and respiration, so the actual rise would be far smaller.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Nothing in the given data produces 45 K.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.6,"rel